$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM (1s/r, 3s/r)-1-(Trifluoromethyl)-3-(trimethylsilyl)cyclobutanol (7a).
This procedure is a modification of the procedure outlined by Prakash et
al5. A 300 mL, 3-necked, round-bottomed flask was equipped with a gas
inlet, septum, stopper and a Teflon-coated stir-bar. The flask was filled
with 100 mL of THF and 18.9 mL (18.2 g, 0.128 mol, 1.4 equiv) of TMS-CF3,
and the mixture was stirred under an argon atmosphere and cooled to 0 °C
in an ice-water bath. Compound 4 (13.0 g, 0.0915 mol) was added via a
syringe and the mixture was allowed to stir for five minutes to ensure
complete mixing. Tetra-n-butylammonium fluoride (TBAF) (0.92 mL, 0.00092
mol, 1M in THF and 5% w/w. H2O, 0.01 equiv) was then syringed into the
mixture, whereupon immediate gas evolution was noted and the solution
became an orange color. (Caution: Although not a problem on small scales,
when the reaction was performed on this rather large scale, initial gas
evolution was such that the stopper had to be held to prevent the flask
from opening. If necessary, we suggest cooling the initial mixture
further to alleviate this problem.) The orange solution was allowed to
warm to room temperature and stir for 30 minutes, whereupon a small
aliquot was withdrawn, added to a vial of CH2Cl2 and checked by GC-MS for
completeness.a The ketone was seen to be completely converted to the
intermediate silyl ether.b In order to cleave the intermediate silyl
ether, the flask was placed in an ice-bath for five minutes, and 7.4 mL
(0.41 mol, 4.48 equiv) of H2O and an additional 5.0 mL of TBAF solution
(0.005 mol, 1M in THF and 5% w/w. H2O, 0.055 equiv) were added via
syringe. The flask was removed from the ice-bath and allowed to stir
overnight, whereupon GC-MS indicated complete conversion to 7a. Upon
completion, approximately 1-2 grams of solid sodium chloride were added
to the reaction mixture and it was briefly stirred to assist separation
of the THF from the water. Solid NaCl remained in the bottom of the
flask. The stirrer was shut off, and the liquid was decanted into a
separatory funnel, along with 150 mL of ether, using some of the ether to
rinse the salt left in the flask. The mixture was then washed with brine
( 1 X 100 mL), and the two layers separated. The brine layer was
back-extracted with additional ether (3 X 25 mL), and these extracts were
added to the first organic layer. The combined organic layers were dried
with Na2SO4 and gravity-filtered through filter paper into a round bottom
flask, using pentane to rinse the Na2SO4. The solvent was removed i n
vacuo via rotary evaporation yielding crude 7a as an orange oil. The
crude product was purified by vacuum distillationc (bp 78-81 °C @ 30 mm
Hg). In order to help prevent solidification of the product in the
condenser, a submersible pump in a bucket of 30 °C water was used to
circulate water through the condenser. A total of 15.6 g of 7a was
collected as a colorless oil, which formed a white crystalline solid upon
standing (80%). mp 35-36 °C. Ratio (1s, 3s) to (1r, 3r) 94:6 as confirmed
by GC-MS and integration of 19F NMR. The following data is for the 94:6
mixture of (1s, 3s) to (1r, 3r) isomers. For NMR, in cases where the
minor isomer was distinguishable, appropriate peaks are indicated. For
GC-MS two peaks were clearly observed in the chromatogram; only the MS
for the major isomer is reported. 1H NMR (CDCl3, 300 MHz) (1s, 3s) δ
-0.006 (9H, s), 1.38-1.52 (1H, m), 1.95-2.03 (2H, m), 2.48-2.56 (2H, m),
2.89 (1H, s, OH); (1r, 3r) δ -0.02 (9H, s, Me3Si), other peaks for the
(1r, 3r) isomer were too small to be seen or were obscured by the other
isomer; 13C NMR (CDCl3, 75 MHz) (1s, 3s) δ -3.93 (CH3), 11.36 (CH), 31.14
(CH2, q, J C-C-C-F = 1.8 Hz), 73.19 (C, q, J C-C-F = 31.0 Hz), 126.07
(CF3, q, J C-F = 282 Hz); (1r, 3r) δ CH3(Si) obscured by major isomer,
13.25 (CH), 29.38 (CH2, q, J C-C-C-F = 2.2 Hz), 74.80 (C, q, J C-C-F =
32.2 Hz), 124.71 (CF3, q, J C-F = 282 Hz); 19F NMR (CDCl3, 282 MHz, TFE =
-79.4 ppm) (1s, 3s) δ -87.88; (1r, 3r) δ -86.78; GC-MS (EI) (1s, 3s) 212
([M]+, 0.012%), 197 ([M-CH3]+, 1.5%), 122, 10.6%) 120 (21.3%), 103
(13.6%), 79 (19.7%), 75 (48.9 %), 73 (100%), 69(21.8%), 59 (11.2%), 45
(20.0%), 43 (17.2%); FTIR (cm-1, neat, ATR) 3312 (m, br, OH), 2956 (m),
1362 (w), 1248 (m), 1157 (vs), 1115 (m), 831 (s), 734 (m); HRMS (ESI),
calcd for C7H12OF3Si [M-CH3]+ : 197.0610, found 197.0611.
